"euron-genai-mastery-30": {
  answer:
`Retrieval quality dropping after an embedding-model switch is almost always one of a few concrete issues — and the #1 suspect is the most basic.

**1. Did you re-embed the documents? (The classic mistake.)** Embeddings from different models live in *different, incompatible vector spaces*. If you switched the model for *queries* but your stored *documents* are still embedded with the old model, you're comparing vectors from two different spaces — retrieval becomes near-random. **The whole corpus must be re-embedded with the new model.** This is the first thing I'd check, and it's the most common cause.

**2. Dimension / metric mismatch.** Different models output different vector **dimensions** and may assume a different **distance metric** (cosine vs dot vs L2) or **normalization**. If the index config, dimension, or metric wasn't updated to match the new model, similarity scores are meaningless. Verify the index was rebuilt with the correct dimension and metric, and that normalization matches what the model expects.

**3. The new model is genuinely worse for *your domain*.** Even done correctly, a model that scores higher on generic benchmarks (MTEB) can be worse on *your* specific domain (legal, medical, code, your jargon). Benchmark ≠ your data. I'd evaluate on a **domain-specific eval set** (real queries with known-relevant docs, measure precision/recall@K) comparing old vs new — don't trust leaderboards over your own data.

**4. Different input conventions.** Some models need **task-specific prefixes/instructions** (e.g. 'query:' vs 'passage:' for asymmetric models like E5/BGE). If the new model expects prefixes and you didn't add them, retrieval degrades. Check the model's documented usage.

**5. Preprocessing / truncation differences.** Different max token length or tokenization — if the new model truncates chunks differently, content gets lost.

**Diagnose & recover:**
- Confirm docs *and* queries use the **same new model** (re-embed the corpus — this fixes most cases).
- Verify dimension, metric, normalization, and any required prefixes match the new model's spec; rebuild the index accordingly.
- Run an **A/B eval on a labeled domain set** (precision@K) old vs new. If new is genuinely worse for your domain → roll back, or fine-tune/choose a better-fit model.
- Keep the ability to **roll back** to the old embeddings quickly (versioned indexes) so a bad switch isn't an outage.

The headline: **re-embed everything with the new model, match dimension/metric/prefix conventions, and validate on your own domain eval set — never just swap the query model or trust a generic benchmark.**`,
  pros:[
    "Pinpoints the #1 cause fast: corpus not re-embedded in the new model's space",
    "Domain eval set reveals whether the new model genuinely fits your data vs benchmarks",
    "Versioned indexes enable instant rollback if the switch is bad"
  ],
  cons:[
    "Re-embedding 100K+ docs is time-consuming and costly",
    "Building a labeled domain eval set takes effort but is essential",
    "Model-specific quirks (prefixes, metrics) are easy to miss in docs"
  ],
  useCase:
`After switching to a 'better' embedding model, search quality cratered. Two issues: the document corpus was still embedded with the old model (mismatched vector spaces), and the new model (BGE-style) required 'query:'/'passage:' prefixes that weren't added. Re-embedding the corpus with the new model and adding the prefixes restored quality — confirmed by a precision@5 comparison on a labeled query set.`,
  alts:[
    "Retrieval got worse after changing embedding models — how do you fix it?",
    "What goes wrong when you swap embedding models in a RAG system?",
    "How do you diagnose and recover from a bad embedding-model switch?"
  ]
},

